  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "This LED bulb produces bright light while using much less power than an old incandescent bulb. Its light comes from tiny semiconductor chips, not from a glowing metal filament. The bulb's driver changes household electricity into a controlled direct current. Inside each LED, electrons and holes move toward a junction in the semiconductor. When they recombine, they release energy as photons, which are particles of light. The chip's materials set the light's colour and a phosphor coating can create white light. A diffuser spreads that light evenly while the metal body carries heat away. So controlled current powers semiconductor junctions that turn electrical energy directly into light.",
